The thesis

    For 70 years, we have treated physical quantities as "naked floats".  We should
    instead treat these as "semantic quantities" so they carry both a numerical type AND
    a semantic type.  

    The math:  physical and semantic units are objects in a free Abelian group.  A group is
    a set of elements with a binary operation * that combines two elements of the group;
    there's an identity element and each element has an inverse.  Being Abelian means the
    group operation is commutative, so that a * b = b * a.  The operation is also
    associative.  Thus, when combining elements, the ordering and grouping of the elements
    doesn't affect the result.  This group also has a basis, which is a subset such that
    every element in the group can be uniquely expressed as an integer combination of
    finitely many basis elements.  These free abelian groups are similar to vector spaces.

    For engineers, a useful mental model is to think of the units of a numerical quantity as
    a vector.  For example, a newton's components in SI is kg*m/s^2.  In mass, length, time
    notation, this is M*L*T⁻².  If you have two numbers a and b, they can only be added if 
    they have the same units:  adding "3 s" to "5 kg" is like adding apples to oranges, it
    just doesn't make sense.  The dimensional algebra of addition requires the "vectors"
    to be the same, otherwise the sum doesn't make sense.  With multiplication, of course,
    you can combine apples and oranges; the user has to decide if apples*oranges makes
    sense.

    This vector space mental model is useful because once you combine a number and a
    unit, you have an invariant element.  Mike and I are calling this the "Noether
    invariant" after Emmy Noether, whose "first theorem" relates a symmetry
    transformation to a conserved quantity, an important principle in physics.  For
    example, a system that looks the same under a rotational transformation (say, a
    bicycle wheel) has angular momentum as a conserved quantity.  Linear translational
    symmetry implies conservation of linear momentum.  Translational symmetry in time
    implies conservation of energy.  Invariants are powerful notions, both in physics
    and computing.

    Here, the conserved quantity (Noether invariant) is, say, "5 m".  Its symmetry
    transformation is a dilatation, also called a scaling transformation.  These are
    fancy words for changing this length to "16.4 ft", done by multiplying by the
    conversion factor 1 m = 3.28 ft.  Note "1 m"/"3.28 ft" is a scalar, a number with no
    units.  Transformations don't affect scalars.

    Since "5 m" is an invariant, when the unit changes, the number also has to change to
    keep it an invariant.  From making a zillion unit errors in calculations myself over
    the last 50+ years, I would hazard a guess that many of the software bugs in the
    world are of the same type.  When I reflect on much of the software I have written,
    the problems I solve involve these Noether invariants frequently rather than bare
    scalars.  I'll bet everyone else's software algorithms use these Noether invariants
    too.

    This leads us to the egregious type error compilers and interpreters have been
    committing for 70 years:  the naked float 5.0 is missing its unit -- it should be
    "5 m".  We know how to do the algebra with these invariants:  if the algebraic
    operations fail, we know there's an error in the logic.

    In other words, these are primitive category errors. A float is a mathematical
    abstraction; a physical quantity is a value + a coordinate in a free Abelian group. 
    By stripping the units, we discard the very metadata that prevents expensive bugs.

    The same unit logic that could have saved the Mars Climate Orbiter in 1999 (a
    monetary loss of half a billion in 2026 dollars) could save an ERP system from a
    million-dollar reconciliation error.

